\section{The path from symbolic reference to executing code}

A JVM implementation is not just an interpreter. In Lindholm et al.'s \emph{Java Virtual Machine Specification}, loading creates a class or interface, linking verifies and prepares it and may resolve symbolic references, and initialization executes its initialization method when required. Preparation establishes default values for static fields; it is not an execution of Java's static initializer. Resolution may occur lazily subject to the specification's rules for observable errors. A class name alone is not its run-time identity: the defining loader matters. A method cache keyed only by spelling can therefore return a method belonging to the wrong class.

A method may start with an interpreter entry and later obtain baseline or optimized code. A profile can suggest a receiver type at a virtual call, but the optimized path needs a guard or an invalidatable assumption. If an assumption fails, execution must recover a state that can continue with ordinary dispatch, not replay work that already completed. Ga\"el Thomas and colleagues describe a different motivation for replacing running code in \emph{Designing highly flexible virtual machines: the JnJVM experience}: adaptation through components and dynamic aspect weaving. Whether replacement responds to observed receiver types or an explicit adaptation request, a new entry can coexist with old frames. Return addresses into the old code still need that code's unwind and collector metadata until those frames are gone.

\section{Representations change what a benchmark measures}

Kris Venstermans, Lieven Eeckhout, and Koen De Bosschere's \emph{64-bit versus 32-bit Virtual Machines for Java} compared a PowerPC system in both modes using Jikes Research VM and an IBM production JVM. Their study separated the contributions of pointers, headers, and alignment to object size, and used hardware counters to investigate cache behavior. The results describe those VMs and workloads, not all modern JVMs. In particular, a runtime can use a narrower heap reference than its native pointer width. Record actual object layout, reference encoding, collector, and heap limits rather than treating the mode label as an explanation.

For a controlled comparison, keep program input, runtime build, hardware and output checks fixed where possible. Collect process-start-to-first-result latency across independent launches, then separately measure steady throughput after recording tier activity. Record compilation time, allocation rate, live heap estimates, pauses, and resident memory; resident memory includes much more than the Java heap. Pair a fixed-byte heap experiment with a heap-size sweep so a runtime doesn't appear slower merely because the same numeric heap limit leaves it with less usable headroom. Report distributions across launches. Iterations from one long-lived JVM can share compilation history and are not independent process samples.

\section{Worked experiment: fewer calls, slower startup}

Suppose two builds run the same verified request workload. Build A processes a request in $20\,\mu\mathrm{s}$ throughout. Build B pays an additional $30\,\mathrm{ms}$ to compile during warm-up and then needs $15\,\mu\mathrm{s}$ per request. Ignoring all other differences, the new tier recovers its cost after $30{,}000/5=6{,}000$ requests \emph{after} compilation. A service restarted after 1,000 optimized requests loses $25\,\mathrm{ms}$ in this model; a long-lived batch may benefit. This break-even calculation is a prediction to test. Background compilation can compete for a core, and GC or code cache usage can change the slope. Log timestamps for compilation, first optimized entry, and workload completion, then compare equal batches on freshly started processes as well as a sustained run.

Add a correctness experiment alongside the throughput run. Warm a call site using a single concrete implementation, then call it with another allowed receiver whose method returns a distinguishable value. The optimized run and interpreter should produce the same sequence of return values and visible side effects, including during any invalidation or fallback. Compare checksums computed from useful outputs, not just counts of completed iterations; if the program does less work, higher throughput is not evidence of a better tier.

\Cref{fig:visual-244} plots the cumulative difference from Build A: crossing zero means repaying the compilation cost, not simply running a faster tier.

