\subsection{Separate Class Preparation from Initialization in Tests}

A test that starts the timer only on entry to a hot Java method
can conflate class initialization and compilation history.
Build a single-threaded fixture with one freshly defined class
per trial, no hierarchy dependencies beyond an initialized
superclass, and no initializer recursion or exceptions. Give it a
non-final static integer and an initializer that records a marker
and assigns 12. Expose separate instrumentation hooks for
preparation and completion of initialization. Read raw prepared
state only in the runtime instrumentation; a Java-level read
of that field is an active use and would change the very state
being observed.

\begin{sudocode}[Measure an active use with an initialization probe]
\State $C\gets$ define fresh fixture class in an isolated loader
\State install probes scoped to defining identity of $C$
\State request linking without initialization
  \Comment{Do not read the field from Java}
\State require prepare probe reports default value 0
\State require observation log empty
\State $a\gets$ invoke static getter of $C$
  \Comment{First active use initializes before executing}
\State $b\gets$ invoke same getter again
\State require $(a,b)=(12,12)$
\State require observation log $=[\mathsf{init}]$
\State record initialization and tier events separately
\end{sudocode}

Suppose the first getter takes $80\,\mu\mathrm{s}$ and the second
requires $5\,\mu\mathrm{s}$, with the probes recording
$70\,\mu\mathrm{s}$ in the initializer. The pair by itself does not
show the getter was optimized. Repeat with the fixture initialized
before both timed calls and collect compilation events. As an
identity check, run the same spelling through a second defining
loader with the initializer assigning 23; expect two independent
initialization markers and results 12 and 23 for their respective
classes.

The invariant in this fixture is one completed initialization
before any successful active use per class identity, not per
spelling or installed method version. Do not generalize the
single-threaded test to the JVM's synchronization and exceptional
initialization rules. Use the instrumented run to explain phases
and the minimally instrumented run to estimate their cost. Retain
the class identity in the event log even when method names agree.

\section{Reporting can change execution}

Heavy event tracing can alter compilation decisions and cache behavior. Compare minimally instrumented timing runs with diagnostic runs, and explain if the two runs exhibit different tier transitions. Chapter~31 of the Modern Compiler Dossier, \emph{Causal Profiling IR}, distinguishes the observation that a site is hot from evidence that it controls elapsed time. A measured improvement only matters for the workload and metric for which it was tested.
